    id: "find-site", section: "site", title: "Find your park",
    body: "This is where you bring in the real place. It's one full-screen map. Search for the park, check the frame around it, pick what comes in, and Look first pulls it all in to look at before Bring it in. It's a starting point. It has not personally visited your park, so expect some cleanup.\n\nOn a phone the panel is a sheet over the map. Drag it down and the map has the whole screen, to move round and set the frame; Look first and Bring it in stay at its top, in reach at any height.\n\nThe search box takes a park's name, an address, a lat, lon or a map link. Disc golf courses and parks come first in the list, and places near the map come before ones far away. A lat, lon or a link finds the spot and, if OpenStreetMap has a park or a course there, names it after that, so the course gets the park's name. If there's nothing named there, it's just the spot. The button under the map's zoom buttons (Go to where I am) frames about 1,500 × 1,000 ft around where you are.\n\nOnce you pick a park, the map frames it by its own outline. Whole park (or The course, when it's a disc golf course) is the frame around all of it. Draw lets you drag across the map to draw your own instead. You can also drag the frame's corners to resize it, or Shift+drag to draw a new one. The line under its size says the same. The frame can be up to 6,000 ft each way, and its size turns red when it's too big. Under 40 ft each way is too small. Esc, or Draw again, stops drawing. With no park picked, Draw the area does the same.\n\nWhat comes in is a row of switches: Photo (with its date and how sharp it is), Ground, Measured trees (with the year of the survey), Trees from the photo where no survey covers the site, Paths, roads and more from OpenStreetMap, and Holes when a disc golf course is mapped there. Bring it in asks for all of it at once.\n\nWhile it comes in, the map fills in as each part lands. A checklist shows what's done and what's still coming, with a ring for how far along it is. If one part fails, the rest still comes in, and the Site step says what's missing. Stop ends all of it right away. Nothing from that try is kept, and you can bring the site in again straight away.\n\nWhen it's done, the site shows in 3D with a card of what came in. Look around lets you fly around it first. Start designing takes you to the Design step. The Site step's Site section then shows its size, area, lowest point and climb, where the elevation and the photo came from, and when the photo was taken, and What came in lists the rest. To change the area later, use Change the area, then Bring it in again.",
